questions.gold_raw:
If point $T$ is placed at $(2,0)$, then $T$ is on $OB$ and $AT$ is perpendicular to $OB$. [asy]
size(5cm);defaultpen(fontsize(9));
pair o = (0, 0); pair q = (0, 12); pair b = (12, 0);
pair a = (2, 12); pair t = (2, 0);

draw((-2, 0)--(15, 0), Arrow);
draw((0, -2)--(0, 15), Arrow);
draw(q--a--b);
draw(a--t);

label("$Q(0, 12)$", q, W);
label("$A(2, 12)$", a, NE);
label("$B(12, 0)$", b, S);
label("$O(0, 0)$", o, SW);
label("$x$", (15, 0), E);
label("$y$", (0, 15), N);
label("$T(2, 0)$", t, S + 0.6 * E);
[/asy] Since $QO$ is perpendicular to $OB$, then $QO$ is parallel to $AT$. Both $QA$ and $OT$ are horizontal, so then $QA$ is parallel to $OT$. Therefore, $QATO$ is a rectangle. The area of rectangle $QATO$ is $QA\times QO$ or $(2-0)\times(12-0)=24$.

Since $AT$ is perpendicular to $TB$, we can treat $AT$ as the height of $\triangle ATB$ and $TB$ as the base. The area of $\triangle ATB$ is $$\frac{1}{2}\times TB\times AT = \frac{1}{2}\times(12-2)\times(12-0)=\frac{1}{2}\times10\times12=60.$$The area of $QABO$ is the sum of the areas of rectangle $QATO$ and $\triangle ATB$, or $24+60=\boxed{84}$.

When using the distributive property for the first time, we add the product of $2p^2 - 1$ and $3p^2$ to the product of $2p^2 - 1$ and $4$:

\begin{align*}
(2p^2 - 1)(3p^2 + 4) &= (2p^2 - 1) \cdot (3p^2) + (2p^2 - 1) \cdot 4
\end{align*}We use the distributive property again and combine like terms:

\begin{align*}
(3p^2)(2p^2 - 1) + 4(2p^2 - 1) &= 6p^4 - 3p^2 + 8p^2 - 4\\
&= \boxed{6p^4 + 5p^2 - 4}
\end{align*}

